WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.025
When working with
tables in a database,

2
00:00:02.025 --> 00:00:03.510
you may often encounter tables

3
00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:05.100
that contain repetitive data.

4
00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:08.385
Perhaps two columns contain
values that are very similar.

5
00:00:08.385 --> 00:00:11.865
You might split the table in
two to simplify the data.

6
00:00:11.865 --> 00:00:14.205
When building
relational databases,

7
00:00:14.205 --> 00:00:15.840
you can solve similar issues of

8
00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:17.490
repetitive data using what's

9
00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:21.310
known as third
normal form, or 3NF.

10
00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:23.035
By the end of this video,

11
00:00:23.035 --> 00:00:24.830
you'll be able to
understand how to design

12
00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:26.720
a database in third normal form,

13
00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:29.734
and explain the concept
of transitive dependency.

14
00:00:29.734 --> 00:00:32.180
Before we begin, make
sure that you've watched

15
00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:34.825
the videos in first and
second normal form.

16
00:00:34.825 --> 00:00:36.290
A database must be in

17
00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:37.835
first and second normal form

18
00:00:37.835 --> 00:00:39.965
before it can be in
third normal form.

19
00:00:39.965 --> 00:00:41.810
In addition to these rules,

20
00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:43.310
databases can contain

21
00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:45.934
any instances of
transitive dependency.

22
00:00:45.934 --> 00:00:48.320
In the context of
third normal form,

23
00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:50.150
transitive dependency means that

24
00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:52.070
a non-key attribute cannot be

25
00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:54.800
functionally dependent on
another non-key attribute.

26
00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:57.260
In other words,
non-key attributes

27
00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:59.320
cannot depend upon one another.

28
00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:02.330
A key attribute in a database
is an attribute that

29
00:01:02.330 --> 00:01:05.525
helps to uniquely identify
a raw of data in a table.

30
00:01:05.525 --> 00:01:07.385
To demonstrate this concept,

31
00:01:07.385 --> 00:01:09.380
let's take the example
of a basic table

32
00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:10.570
with three columns;

33
00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:14.150
A, B, and C. The concept of

34
00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:16.100
transitive dependency means that

35
00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:18.760
the value of A determines
the value of B.

36
00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:21.870
Likewise, the value of B
determines the value of C.

37
00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:24.380
The relation between
these table columns

38
00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:25.760
is represented by A,

39
00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:30.970
B, and C. This means that
A determines C through B.

40
00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:32.840
This is the type
of relation that

41
00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:35.810
database engineers called
transitive dependency.

42
00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:38.750
Let's see how this works
using a more complex example.

43
00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:41.060
I have a table of
best-selling books

44
00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:43.925
within Europe from the database
of an online bookstore.

45
00:01:43.925 --> 00:01:46.100
The table organizes
the books according to

46
00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:48.830
five attributes:
Book ID and title,

47
00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:52.330
author name, author
language, and country.

48
00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:54.800
In this table, ID is the only

49
00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:57.815
key or primary key that
exists in the table.

50
00:01:57.815 --> 00:02:00.800
All other attributes
are non-key attributes.

51
00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:03.550
But to determine what these
non-key attributes are,

52
00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:06.475
I most used the ID of
the top-selling books.

53
00:02:06.475 --> 00:02:08.165
This means if I want to find

54
00:02:08.165 --> 00:02:10.940
any specific information
about any attribute,

55
00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:13.040
I need to use the
ID attribute value

56
00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:15.100
to find the targeted
attribute value.

57
00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:18.560
For example, if I
use the ID of 3,

58
00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:20.840
then I can locate the
author, Cormac O'Dwyer,

59
00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:22.700
the language,
Irish, the country,

60
00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:24.170
Ireland, and so on.

61
00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:25.790
However, it's also possible to

62
00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:28.115
determine the country
based on the language,

63
00:02:28.115 --> 00:02:31.010
or to determine the language
based on the country,

64
00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:34.895
and both country and language
are non-key attributes.

65
00:02:34.895 --> 00:02:36.860
For example, in the
context of Europe,

66
00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:39.905
it's always possible to
determine the country is France,

67
00:02:39.905 --> 00:02:42.425
if the language is
French, and vice versa.

68
00:02:42.425 --> 00:02:44.150
This means that I
have a transitive

69
00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:45.470
dependency in this relation.

70
00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:49.775
A non-key attribute depends
on another non-key attribute.

71
00:02:49.775 --> 00:02:52.340
This dependency relation
can be presented as follows

72
00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:54.905
: Language determines country,

73
00:02:54.905 --> 00:02:56.995
and country determines language.

74
00:02:56.995 --> 00:02:59.390
The rest of the attributes
are fine as they

75
00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:02.035
only depend on the
ID primary key.

76
00:03:02.035 --> 00:03:03.740
You can't say, for instance,

77
00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:06.140
that author name
determines book title,

78
00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:08.840
or that author name
determines language.

79
00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:10.580
For example, the author,

80
00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:12.440
Michel Leiris has
written two books in

81
00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:15.275
two different languages,
French and Spanish.

82
00:03:15.275 --> 00:03:18.350
As I've just pointed out, the
only transitive dependency

83
00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:19.775
that exists in this example

84
00:03:19.775 --> 00:03:21.635
is between language and country.

85
00:03:21.635 --> 00:03:24.380
How do I solve this
transitive dependency within

86
00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:27.245
my table and remove any
repetition of data?

87
00:03:27.245 --> 00:03:29.750
I can split the table
into two tables while

88
00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:32.900
joining them to conform
with 3NF rules.

89
00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:35.465
I keep the top books table,

90
00:03:35.465 --> 00:03:37.070
while splitting off
the country and

91
00:03:37.070 --> 00:03:39.980
language columns into a
new table called country.

92
00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:42.350
But I also leave the
country column inside

93
00:03:42.350 --> 00:03:43.730
the top books table as

94
00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:45.965
a foreign key that
connects the two tables.

95
00:03:45.965 --> 00:03:47.510
The country table now holds

96
00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:50.295
just four records with
no repetition of data.

97
00:03:50.295 --> 00:03:52.070
There's no need for
language column

98
00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:53.680
within the top books table.

99
00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:56.870
Stating the country is enough
to determine the language.

100
00:03:56.870 --> 00:03:59.735
Most importantly, all
non-key attributes

101
00:03:59.735 --> 00:04:02.570
are determined only by the
primary key in each table.

102
00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:04.400
This means that my
table then meets

103
00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:06.605
the requirements of 3NF.

104
00:04:06.605 --> 00:04:08.360
You should now
know how to design

105
00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:10.495
a database in third normal form.

106
00:04:10.495 --> 00:04:12.980
You should also be able
to explain the concept of

107
00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:16.410
transitive dependency.
Well done.